          production: "MINING: Porphyry copper deposits (Chile, Peru) are the world's largest. Low-grade ore (0.3\u20131.5% Cu) is mined via large open pits. High-grade deposits include SEDEX (Congo) and skarn deposits. Ore is crushed and ground to <200 micron. FLOTATION: Ground ore is mixed with water, collectors (xanthate), and frothers. Air is injected; copper sulfide minerals attach to bubbles and float to surface as concentrate (~25\u201330% Cu). SMELTING: Concentrate is dried, then flash-smelted in an Outotec furnace at 1200\xB0C \u2014 partial oxidation produces copper matte (60\u201370% Cu). Matte is converted in a Peirce-Smith converter, then fire-refined to blister copper (98.5% Cu). ELECTROLYTIC REFINING: Blister copper is cast as anodes, electrolyzed in CuSO\u2084/H\u2082SO\u2084 solution. Pure copper deposits on starter sheets as 99.99% cathode copper. Anode slimes contain gold, silver, platinum group metals. HYDROMETALLURGY: Low-grade oxide ores are heap-leached with dilute H\u2082SO\u2084, then copper is recovered by solvent extraction-electrowinning (SX-EW) to produce cathodes without smelting.",
